{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His life story involves complex political maneuvering alongside significant military engagements against rising Islamic powers in the region. He acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and this position placed him at the center of royal succession disputes that eventually weakened the crusader states.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond inherited his title after his father Raymond II was assassinated by the Order of Assassins in 1152. He was only a young boy when he assumed control over the County of Tripoli so guardians managed affairs until he reached maturity. His early reign focused on stabilizing his borders while navigating alliances with neighboring crusader lords and the Byzantine Empire. He married Eschiva of Bures which brought him additional lands and influence within the kingdom yet this union also drew him deeper into internal factional conflicts.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe period of his regency marked a time of intense rivalry between two distinct factions within the kingdom. One group supported Guy of Lusignan while Raymond led the opposing faction that favored maintaining stability through careful diplomacy. He opposed the aggressive policies of Renaud of Châtillon because he believed such actions would provoke Saladin into full-scale war. Raymond sought peace treaties and truces to buy time for the fragile Christian territories but his efforts were often undermined by warmongering elements within the court who ignored his counsel.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Legacy*\nTensions culminated in the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader army faced total defeat against the forces of Saladin. Raymond commanded a portion of the Christian forces during this engagement but he escaped the battlefield while many others perished or were captured. Historians debate whether his retreat was an act of cowardice or a strategic necessity given the hopeless situation. He died shortly after the battle possibly from illness or despair over the loss of much of the kingdom including Jerusalem itself. His death ended a major political era and left the crusader states without one of their most experienced leaders during their darkest hour. The consequences of his decisions and the failures of his opponents shaped the remaining history of the Latin East for decades to come.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.710288e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0004940061}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0023330823}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.18271562}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.7881409e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -3.8266913e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -4.4107533e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.218656e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.08481045e-05}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -1.4305125e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0030926554}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.1173543}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.011997078}, {"token": " role", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " within", "logprob": -0.29575026}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Kingdom", "logprob": -0.03346